<crash-analysis>
The crash is caused by a bug in the `psf_strlcpy_crlf` function in `common.c`. The function does not correctly handle the case where the last character of the source buffer is a carriage return (`\r`) or a newline (`\n`).

Specifically, the line:
`if ((src [0] == '\r' && src [1] == '\n') || (src [0] == '\n' && src [1] == '\r'))`
accesses `src[1]` without checking if `src + 1` is within the bounds of the source buffer (`srcend`). If `src` points to the last byte of the source buffer, this will result in a one-byte read overflow.

To fix this, the loop condition should be changed to `while (dest < destend && src < srcend - 1)` when checking for `\r\n` or `\n\r` pairs, and then handle the last byte separately.

A possible patch would be to add a check before accessing `src[1]`:

```c
void
psf_strlcpy_crlf (char *dest, const char *src, size_t destmax, size_t srcmax)
{	/* Must be minus 2 so it can still expand a single trailing '\n' or '\r'. */
char * destend = dest + destmax - 2 ;
const char * srcend = src + srcmax ;

while (dest < destend && src < srcend)
{	if (src + 1 < srcend && ((src [0] == '\r' && src [1] == '\n') || (src [0] == '\n' && src [1] == '\r')))
{	*dest++ = '\r' ;
*dest++ = '\n' ;
src += 2 ;
continue ;
} ;

if (src [0] == '\r')
{	*dest++ = '\r' ;
*dest++ = '\n' ;
src += 1 ;
continue ;
} ;

if (src [0] == '\n')
{	*dest++ = '\r' ;
*dest++ = '\n' ;
src += 1 ;
continue ;
} ;

*dest++ = *src++ ;
} ;

/* Make sure dest is terminated. */
*dest = 0 ;
} /* psf_strlcpy_crlf */
```
</crash-analysis>

